{"text": "This section includes only information that changes how support should be provided. For Aaliyah, the pertinent information is: sensory: light sensitivity and visual fatigue; mobility: needs preview in unfamiliar settings; medical: protective sun/eye measures; and behaviour: no primary concern. These details belong in the profile because they affect access, safety, regulation, communication, or independence. They should be shared only with staff who need the information and handled respectfully.\n\nThe priority challenges are the barriers we have already described: glare and low contrast; copying from the board; small labels and crowded worksheets; and fast ball games and unfamiliar routes. The EA’s role is not to diagnose, redesign the student’s program independently, or provide constant help. The EA works under teacher direction and follows the IEP, health and safety plans, behaviour-support plans, and specialist recommendations.\n\nFor Aaliyah, the most useful EA actions are to check access instead of guessing; describe demonstrations aloud; maintain clear travel paths; and coordinate materials with the vision teacher. In practice, this means preparing access before the activity, watching for the student’s communication, using the least intrusive prompt that works, and fading support when the student can take over.\n\nAcross every activity, four priorities remain constant: dignity, safety, access, and growing independence. We can now use the student’s actual learning goals to decide whether the program is adapted or modified.", "voice": "en-GB-SoniaNeural", "audio": "special-education-E-05.mp3", "words": [{"text": "This", "start": 0.1375, "end": 0.3875, "start_char": 0, "end_char": 4}, {"text": "section", "start": 0.4, "end": 0.8875, "start_char": 5, "end_char": 12}, {"text": "includes", "start": 0.9, "end": 1.475, "start_char": 13, "end_char": 21}, {"text": "only", "start": 1.4875, "end": 1.7625, "start_char": 22, "end_char": 26}, {"text": "information"